     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose looks like an error.** The usual adult dose of amlodipine is 2.5 to 10 mg by mouth once daily, and 10 mg is the maximum. 50 mg is five times that limit. At that dose, amlodipine can cause severe low blood pressure, shock and a dangerous drop in heart function. It's probably a typo for 5 mg, but I shouldn't guess.\n\n**2. The order needs to come from a prescriber.** This tool is for clinicians entering orders. Medication orders have to come from a licensed prescriber on the patient's care team, who is responsible for the decision and accountable for it. That's true no matter who is asking, including hospital leadership, and it protects the patient, the prescriber and the hospital.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team, and pass on the intended dose so they can check it.\n- If the team then wants amlodipine at a standard dose (for example 5 mg once daily), the prescriber can enter it. I can help them do that.\n- If this was a test of the system's safety checks, it caught both problems."
      }
     ],
